<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!-- This is the theme the app actually runs under: Capacitor's BridgeActivity
         swaps to it once the splash is done, so the system-bar items belong HERE
         and not only on the launch theme below.

         The app is dark end to end (the `bg` custom property in styles.css,
         #0b0d10; XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, hence the prose),
         and the platform default leaves a light grey status bar with dark icons
         sitting on top of it, which reads as a rendering fault rather than a
         style choice.

         `windowBackground` is the one that matters now. The shell lays out edge
         to edge and pads the WebView's container by the system-bar insets
         (MainActivity.takeOverWindowInsets), so the strip of screen behind the
         status bar shows this colour and nothing else. Left inherited it is
         AppCompat's dark grey, which against a near black app reads as a band
         someone forgot to paint. It has to be a COLOUR and not a drawable:
         Capacitor's SystemBars plugin copies this attribute onto the decor view
         with `TypedValue.data`, which for a drawable reference is a resource id
         rendered as a colour.

         There is deliberately no `windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement` here any
         more. It kept the pre-Android-15 layout, where the window sat below the
         bars, and it is IGNORED on Android 16 for an app targeting SDK 36 — so
         the same APK laid out one way on one phone and the other way on the
         next, and on the newer phone the app's own top strip ran under the
         clock and the battery. Edge-to-edge is now entered on purpose and the
         insets are handled once, in one place, for every version.

         The parent is DayNight on purpose. From targetSdk 33 the WebView answers
         prefers-color-scheme from this theme's isLightTheme, not from the phone:
         a plain (dark) AppCompat parent made "follow device" read dark on a phone
         set to light. DayNight flips isLightTheme with the phone's night mode;
         the colours below stay pinned, and the page repaints them per theme
         (applyBarAppearance). -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#0B0D10</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">#0B0D10</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#0B0D10</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="27">false</item>
    </style>


    <!-- The app is dark end to end (the `bg` custom property in styles.css,
         #0b0d10; XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, hence the prose).
         Capacitor's
         default theme leaves a LIGHT status bar with dark icons sitting on top
         of it, and the launch window flashes white before the WebView paints —
         both read as a rendering fault rather than a style choice on a phone.
         The splash drawable is regenerated on the same background.

         Same as above: no edge-to-edge opt-out. The splash is a centred drawable
         on a solid background, so it looks identical either way, and having the
         launch window and the app window disagree about whether they are
         edge-to-edge is a visible jump at the handover. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/splash</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">#0B0D10</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#0B0D10</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="27">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
